# Clean Code Rules
- Write clean, readable, and maintainable code
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Keep functions small and focused on a single responsibility
- Avoid deep nesting - prefer early returns and guard clauses
- No magic numbers - use named constants
- Follow consistent naming conventions
- Remove dead code and unused imports
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Write pure functions when possible
- Avoid global variables

# Code Style
- Use consistent indentation and formatting
- Keep line length reasonable (80-120 characters)
- Group related code together
- Separate concerns clearly
- Use whitespace effectively for readability

# No Comments Policy
- Never add comments unless explicitly requested
- Write self-documenting code
- Use meaningful names instead of explanatory comments
- Focus on "what" the code does through clear naming
- Avoid TODO, FIXME, or explanatory comments

# Error Handling
- Handle errors gracefully
- Use specific error types
- Fail fast when appropriate
- Don't ignore exceptions

# Performance & Best Practices
- Avoid premature optimization
- Use appropriate data structures
- Minimize code duplication (DRY principle)
- Follow language-specific best practices
- Consider memory usage and performance implications